</pre></td></tr></tbody></table></code></pre></div></div>

<p>As you can see, the system registers 5 <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Mode</code>s by default:</p>

<ol>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">kCFRunLoopDefaultMode</code>: the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">App</code>’s default <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Mode</code>; the main thread usually runs in this <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Mode</code>.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">UITrackingRunLoopMode</code>: the UI tracking <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Mode</code>, used by <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ScrollView</code> to track touch scrolling, ensuring that scrolling isn’t affected by other <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Mode</code>s.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">UIInitializationRunLoopMode</code>: the first <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Mode</code> entered when the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">App</code> just starts; it’s no longer used after startup completes.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">GSEventReceiveRunLoopMode</code>: the internal <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Mode</code> for receiving system events; you usually don’t need it.</li>
  <li><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">kCFRunLoopCommonModes</code>: this is a placeholder <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Mode</code> with no actual effect.</li>
</ol>

<p>You can see more of Apple’s internal <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Mode</code>s <a href="http://iphonedevwiki.net/index.php/CFRunLoop">here</a>, but those <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">Mode</code>s are hard to encounter in development.</p>

